WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.024
Heart failure is not a single signal. Physiological

00:00:04.024 --> 00:00:07.090
changes appear through movements, rhythms and

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.542
measurements interpreted in context.

00:00:12.717 --> 00:00:16.205
Non-invasive and minimally invasive sensors can

00:00:16.205 --> 00:00:16.724
produce

00:00:16.724 --> 00:00:20.514
information about cardiac and circulatory physiology

00:00:20.514 --> 00:00:24.011
without requiring identical clinical procedures.

00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:31.183
The chapter surveys sensor technologies intended to

00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:31.455
help

00:00:31.455 --> 00:00:34.926
identify or predict heart-failure trajectories, with

00:00:34.926 --> 00:00:37.262
different technological trade-offs.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.217
Motion, noise, timing and patient variability all

00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:48.184
influence interpretation. A useful measurement is not

00:00:48.184 --> 00:00:51.252
automatically a useful clinical decision.

00:00:54.758 --> 00:00:58.529
Predictive performance needs evidence from realistic

00:00:58.529 --> 00:01:02.155
populations and robust external testing; practical

00:01:02.155 --> 00:01:05.181
accessibility matters alongside technical

00:01:05.181 --> 00:01:06.288
sophistication.

00:01:10.008 --> 00:01:13.434
This 2022 book chapter maps the promise and

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:14.311
constraints

00:01:14.311 --> 00:01:18.458
of measuring physiological warning signs. The chapter

00:01:18.458 --> 00:01:20.962
supplies the cited technologies.
